/*!
\group android
\title Qt for Android
\brief Provides information about Android support in Qt.
\ingroup supportedplatform

Qt has two ways for you to develop applications for Android:

\list
\li \l {qt-for-android-overview}{Qt for Android} for developing full Qt
    applications.
\li \l {qt-quick-for-android-overview}{Qt Quick for Android} for integrating
    Qt Quick content into native Android applications.
\endlist

\section1 \Q4A
\target qt-for-android-overview

Qt for \tm Android enables you to develop Qt applications for Android devices,
and supports a wide range of features and use-cases.

Qt for Android provides the platform integration, runtime bridges, build and
packaging support, and platform plugins that connect the cross-platform Qt
libraries (such as Qt Core, Qt GUI, Qt Quick, and Qt Widgets) to
Android-specific services and the Android SDK.

Qt applications run on Android devices by handling the interaction between the
native Android (Java/Kotlin) side and the Qt (C++) side, including event
propagation, lifecycle management, and rendering.

\image images/android_overview_apps.webp "Qt for Android demo applications running on an Android emulator"

\section2 Key Features and Capabilities

The main features of Qt for Android:

\list
\li \b{Cross-platform development.} A Qt application for Android is typically a
    QML application with the code behind it written in C++. With minimal or no
    adjustments, you can write your application code once and deploy it on
    multiple platforms.
\li \b{Native performance.} Since the Qt runtime is compiled for Android, it
    provides native performance and access to native capabilities of Android
    devices.
\li \b{Rich UI components.} Use Qt's extensive set of \l{Qt Quick} components
    for responsive interfaces.

    You can also use the extensive module ecosystem of Qt to add features to
    your application:
    \list
    \li \l {Qt Quick Controls} for ready-made UI controls that feature a
        customizable \l {Material Style} that adapts to Android's look and feel.
    \li \l {Qt Widgets} for traditional desktop-style widgets, if needed.
    \li \l {Qt Quick 3D} for displaying 3D graphics in your applications.
    \li \l {Qt Quick Shapes} for vector graphics and custom shapes.
    \li \l {Important Concepts In Qt Quick - Graphical Effects}{Particles and graphical effects}
        for advanced visual effects.
    \li \l {Qt Multimedia} for audio and video playback and recording.
    \li \l {Qt Graphs} for 2D and 3D data visualization.
    \li \l {Qt Virtual Keyboard} for on-screen keyboard support.
    \li \l {Qt Sensors} for accessing device sensors.
    \li \l {Qt Location} for mapping and location-based services.
    \li \l {Qt Bluetooth} for Bluetooth connectivity.
    \li \l {Qt Network} for secure network communication and cryptography.
    \endlist
\li \b{C++ integration.} Ideal for applications that rely on existing C++
    libraries or require high-performance native code.
\li \b{Mature and consistent APIs.} Qt's stable modules reduce platform
    fragmentation and make it easier to maintain feature parity between
    platforms.
\endlist

\section2 Explore Qt for Android

To learn about developing with Qt for Android, here are the main topics
to check:

\list
    \li \l {Getting Started with Qt for Android}
    \li \l {Qt for Android Prerequisites}
    \li \l {How Qt for Android Works}
    \li \l {Qt for Android Manifest File Configuration}
    \li \l {Building Qt for Android Projects from Command Line}
    \li \l {Deploying an Application on Android}
    \li \l {The androiddeployqt Tool}
    \li \l {Qt for Android - Building from Source}{Building Qt for Android from Source}
    \li \l {Qt Jenny: Java Code Generator}
\endlist

\section2 Android Fragments

You cannot use \l{Android: Fragments}{Android Fragments} in a Qt for Android
application. However, Qt does offer its own modular components and views with
QML. You can combine multiple components in one QML view. For more information,
see \l{QML Documents}.

If you are integrating \l{Qt Quick for Android} into an Android application, you
can use the \l QtQuickView API inside Android Fragments.

\section1 Qt Quick for Android
\target qt-quick-for-android-overview

The Qt Quick for Android lets you add Qt Quick components to your native
Android application UI as a \l {Android: View}{View}. This is particularly
useful for integrating complex UI elements, animations, or 3D graphics into
specific parts of your app without rewriting the entire application in Qt.
Unlike Qt for Android, which replaces the entire UI with Qt, Qt Quick for
Android complements your existing native Android UI.

With Qt Quick for Android, you can extend your native application's UI with
these components:

    \list
    \li \l {Qt Quick} and \l {Qt Quick Controls} for ready-made UI controls that
        feature a customizable \l {Material Style} that adapts to Android's look
        and feel.
    \li \l {Qt Quick 3D} for displaying 3D graphics in your applications.
    \li \l {Qt Quick Shapes} for vector graphics and custom shapes.
    \li \l {Important Concepts In Qt Quick - Graphical Effects}{Particles and graphical effects}
        for advanced visual effects.
    \li \l {Qt Graphs} for 2D and 3D data visualization.
    \endlist

Qt Quick for Android includes a \l{\QtTAS}{plugin} for Google's Android Studio.
You can find it on the IntelliJ marketplace.

\target android-supported-configurations
\section1 Supported Configurations

\include supported-platforms.qdocinc android
*/

